A||N||11||fropuff||Turbo Charged GT||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||02-20-2001||10:36 AM||||Hey, I'm thinking of turbo charging my 86 GT using a turbo from a 2.3L Ford.  I know that Dennis LaGrua did this with success, but I was wondering who else has done this.  I'm trying to gather information on this.  I've emailed Dennis and Chris West about turbo charging, but I'm wondering what everyone else has to say.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Predator||02-20-2001||11:24 AM||||Good luck with the install. I have a Design One Systems Stage II turbo kit installed on my 85 GT, and it was a job installing it. I can't imagine fabrigating all the plumbing and chip programming involved from scratch. I know people have done it, and I applaud them for having that kind of ability. I guess what I am saying is putting a Turbo in a Fiero "right" is a very complex deal, and as I have found can get very expensive real quick. If the fuel system isn't in great shape you can fry your engine in a heart beat. I found that out the hard way. Let us know if you do it, and how it turns out.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||fropuff||02-20-2001||11:28 AM||||I thought about the exhaust stuff, so I'm wondering if the guy that did Dennis LaGrua's exhaust would do that for mine.  I hope so.  I'm going exactly what Dennis has, so the exhaust would be the exact same for his and mine.  By the way, do I need a turbo for a car with automatic transmission?  That's what I have.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Predator||02-20-2001||01:26 PM||||All my experience is with the kit I have so I can give you my .02 cents worth as far as it goes. The physical Turbo unit in my kit does not care if it is auto or manual. The ECM and exhaust system is different according to weather it is a auto or manual. You will need a new MAP sensor and ECM to handle the fuel curve under boost. My kit included a GM knock sensor which is highly recommended to add. The 85 computer is what you will need if you are going to add a knock sensor. The newer computers don't handle a knock sensor. If you don't you could blow a hole in your pistons without even knowing you have a problem. The knock sensor will retard your timing under boost if the engine starts detonating. The MAP and ECM changes will increase fule under boost and retard the timing according to the engines need. I had to have my heads redone, because I had dirty fuel injectors, and when I installed the turbo it ran lean, and fried the head gasket. If you do this you want to install a fuel/air guage so you can see if you are running lean under boost. That little mistake cost me dearly money wise. Good luck with the upgrade. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hartz||02-20-2001||02:31 PM||||Another thought - I didn't install a knock sensor with my turbo, but I did install an MSD ignition box, with the boost timing master (BTM) retard feature The MSD 6BTM is essentially a version of the 6AL, but it provides an adjustable way to retard timing as boost increases.  I've also got an air/fuel meter in the car, and while the whole process hasn't exactly been painless, it seems to have held together so far (knock on wood!).<P>Best of luck,<BR>Hartz<BR>'85 SE V6 Turbo<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||02-20-2001||04:43 PM||||Predator,<P>The information of the knock sensor is great. This would be my prefered method of controlling the detonation. I also believe that it would actually produce more power because you motor could run on the "ragged edge" of the detonation curve which may not be linear. Using the MSD 6BTM seemed like a "best guess" situation at best.<P>Can you provide a little more details on which knock sensor you used (part number or model of car) and how to complete the interface with the computer? <P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000006||Predator||02-20-2001||07:33 PM||||I'll check the installation manual and see if I can find the part number. I have the instructions. I do know you need a computer from a 85 Fiero for it to work. It is the only one that is setup to accept a knock sensor. For some reason GM took the extra chip out that makes it work in the later models. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BigTurbo87GT||02-21-2001||02:24 PM||||Predator, do you know if there are any other significant differences in the computer between 85 and later ones (specifically the 87, since that's my project). This would be a God send if I could plug in an 85 Computer with a knock sensor. Thank you very much!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Predator||02-22-2001||08:29 AM||||I will check the manual and setup this weekend and try and give you some more information on the parts used. The guy that worked on the car after the install ranted and raved about how simple and well the system is designed. He said if he gets a request for a turbo kit for a Fiero in his shop he will recommend the Design One Systems kit. I have had no more problems with it since the dirty fuel injector mishap causing my head gasket to blow. It wasn't the Turbo kits fault, but the car had been sitting for years, and I didn't take into account that the fuel system would be dirty. If you install a turbo you might want to make sure your fuel injectors are tested first. The knock sensor is the only thing the manual refers to as being different between the 85 and newer Fiero's. It is a simple plug in to install the knock sensor. That was one of the easiest parts of the installation of the kit. The kit new is $4,000 which sounds expensive, but Design One uses good quality GM parts, and the turbo is supposed to be one of the best made you can buy according to Ed at Engineered, and even though I bought the kit off of ebay Kevin the owner of Design One spent a lot time politely helping me with the installation. You actually get a lot for the $4,000. The kit is complete, and prefabed to fit exactly into the Fiero, and there ain't much room to play with back there  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Some of the fittings were really close. If you have a manual transmission you get a new exhaust system with it which sounds pretty good. I will post some pitures of the installed kit if you want. One note I haven't had a problem with heat yet "knock on wood" since I installed the turbo. The engine compartment is hotter, but the engine has not run hot yet, and I have sat in some severe traffic jams. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BigTurbo87GT||02-22-2001||02:28 PM||||Great! Thank you very very much for sharing the info. I can't wait to try this out. I'm actually fabricating my own system, I was given a turbo and I think I know enough about what I'm doing to make it work ;-). The most expensive part was going to be a computer to detect and control knock, but if the 85 computer fits the bill, SWEEET!!!<BR>The other question is, where does the knock sensor mount in the block?<BR>Again, thanks.<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Clean near stocker<BR>01 IS300||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||02-23-2001||10:13 AM||||Ok guys here is the info. The PROM chip in the ECM will need to be modified by a professional. Parts list for a knock sensor upgrade. I will list the part number then what it is. G6869 is the ECM. MWESC is the PROM. I am not sure if you can just buy one. G2621 is the knock module. G7562 is the knock sensor. P3674 is the knock terminal. P5323 is the terminal seal. P5375 is the knock housing. MWH200 wiring harness. If you can't buy this here is a description of what it is. Housing, terminals, seals, 7 feet of brown wire, 1 foot of black wire, 1 foot of red wire, and 1 foot of blue wire. The knock sensor is located on the back side of the engine facing the rear of the car. It replaces a freeze plug, and is just above the oil pan. The knock module sites behind the computer with double stick tape. I hope this helps. Kevin at Design One is a real nice guy, and if you need help he is a great source. If you want to just buy the knock sensor setup and instructions he would probably sell it to you, and reprogram the PROM. He is a good source for this type of information, and can probably tell you what to look for, and what to be aware of as far as possible problems goes. He didn't try to push anything off on me when I talked to him about the kit I bought off of ebay, and went out of his way to help. As I stated before, Ed of Engineered was impressed with the kit that Kevin had designed so he knows what he is doing. He may not pull the most HP out of the engine that you could with a TURBO, but he designed the system to give the most HP with the best reliability. Nothing would be worse than to build a 300 hp turbo system just to have it fry your engine after the first 1,000 miles. From talking to Ed and Kevin the stock 2.8 can't handle more than about 225 hp reliably. If you go more then you have to rebuild your stock fuel system, and have new bigger injectors. To me that is more than enough power coming out of my little Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Before designing and building a kit on your own I would look into how much everything will cost, and see if you are really saving that much money. I found out even with a kit that boost is not an easy thing to add to a car that was not designed for it. I am still trying to figure out if I can even install the intercooler that came with the kit. I am not trying to discourage you from building the turbo. I am just stating a big word of caution. I had to have my heads redone because I did not think of the lean condition created with my dirty fuel injectors. All it takes is one little mistake to cost a lot of money. Good luck, and let us know how it turns out.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000011||BigTurbo87GT||02-23-2001||11:28 AM||||Fantastic! Thank you very much, this is perfect! I have actually talked to Design 1 regarding purchasing just a computer w/knock sensing capability and they are asking $800. As you say, they are very nice and willing to talk to anyone that inquires. 800 is probably fair, but I can also buy a much more versatile/tuneable computer for about that much as well. The tuning is not an easy thing to do with a generic computer and probably wouldn't be worth the effort if I wasn't looking at this as a fun hobby car that I want to take to the absolute limits (of both my knowledge & ability as well as the cars). <BR>Once again, thank you for the excellent info.<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Clean near stocker<BR>01 IS300||||reg||1
